00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:11.070 align:start position:0%
European Union environment ministers  have debated the impact of biofuels on  land use as the EU looks to introduce  new rules to encourage a shift to  biofuels that deliver greater greenhouse  savings as food prices rise and farmers  look to meet demand more land is being  cleared to grow crops that were once  planted in land where biofuels are now  produced The ministers agreed on the  need to address these competing needs  and in particular the negative impact of  biofuels but they differed in their  views on how best to resolve the problem  some raised concerns about the impact of  any changes on current investments and  that they might result in the less  efficient biofuels being imported into  Europe  during their meeting in Russells the  minister's also debated changes to the  way that environmental impact studies  are carried out the goals to improve the  effectiveness of the process without  adding too much bureaucratic red tape  they're in favor of reviewing and  addressing the environmental impact  assessment issues but they want to do so  to ensure that it is streamlined that is  efficient that it doesn't add  administrative burdens and it takes  account of the fact that there's a lot  of different consent processes already  in existence in various member states so  it is a quite a lot of work to be done  the minister's hope to be able to reach  an agreement on the reforms during the  second half of this year  the meeting also took stock of the reach  chemicals regulations five years after  they were introduced most countries  welcomed the European Commission's  attempts to reduce the burden of these  rules concerning hazardous chemicals on  small and medium-sized companies
 

